---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Aurora from '../components/Aurora.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Manifesto — Maxxtopia"
  description="Why Maxxtopia exists. The principles behind the suite — sourced, native, reversible, no telemetry, and built like the tools we wished existed."
>
  <article class="relative">
    <Aurora intensity="low" />
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-grid opacity-30"></div>

    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-2xl px-5 sm:px-8 pt-24 pb-12">
      <p class="text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-[var(--color-magenta)] font-mono reveal reveal-1">
        Manifesto · v0.1
      </p>
      <h1 class="reveal reveal-2 mt-4 text-[44px] sm:text-[68px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] leading-[1] gradient-text">
        Built like the tools <span class="serif-accent gradient-suite">we wished existed.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="reveal reveal-3 mt-6 text-[18px] leading-[1.55] text-[var(--color-text-mid)] max-w-xl">
        Six years of gaming software has been a contest in who can ship a Chromium browser pretending to be an application. We're done.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-2xl px-5 sm:px-8 pb-32">
      <div class="space-y-7 text-[16.5px] leading-[1.78] text-[var(--color-text-mid)]">
        <p class="reveal-on-scroll">
          The tools competitive gamers actually use weren't built by anyone competitive.
          They were built by Discord scripters who taped a Stripe button onto a registry batch file.
          By Electron studios shipping 200 MB of Chromium for a settings panel.
          By analytics-funded SaaS that treats your inputs as inventory.
          <span class="text-[var(--color-text)]">Hone is 200 MB.</span>
          <span class="text-[var(--color-text)]">Paragon is 81 MB.</span>
          The official Discord client opens fourteen connections to six domains the second you launch it.
        </p>

        <p class="reveal-on-scroll">
          Maxxtopia is a refusal.
          The position is simple: gaming utilities should be designed with the care Apple gives a music app.
          A tuner should cite its sources.
          A Discord client should idle at 0% CPU.
          A clip tool should know what actually went viral on TikTok today — not just spike on volume.
          Nothing in your gaming stack should call home unless you told it to.
        </p>

        <p class="reveal-on-scroll">
          And we try until it's right.
          The discordmaxxer logo took five passes to land — chat bubble, headset, monogram, broadcast wave, then the Slice with a magenta plasma seam through the cut.
          Five tries because the first four were generic.
          The fifth wasn't.
          <span class="text-[var(--color-text)]">That's the operating principle.</span>
          Ship the version you'd defend, not the first one that loaded.
        </p>

        <h2 class="reveal-on-scroll text-[var(--color-text)] text-[28px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] pt-6 leading-tight">
          Principles
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-5">
          {[
            { t: 'Sourced. Or it doesn\'t ship.', b: "Every tweak ships with a primary source — Microsoft Learn, vendor whitepaper, published kernel reference. If we can't cite it, it doesn't make the build. We cut ten placebo tweaks in v0.1.32 alone for failing this rule." },
            { t: 'Native or nothing.', b: 'The tuner is 3.6 MB. Discordmaxxer idles at 288 MB versus 1,041 MB on stock. Bundle size is a moral position. Electron is 200 MB of Chromium pretending to be an application — we don\'t ship that to you to make our build easier.' },
            { t: 'Reversible by default.', b: 'Every change is snapshot-backed. Every preset is undoable. One-click full restore. Software you can experiment with because the door is never locked behind a paid tier.' },
            { t: 'No telemetry. Ever.', b: "We don't analytics you. We don't error-report you. We don't 'help us improve' you. Gaming software has a long history of betraying that trust — Razer, Logitech, half the AIO control panels. We won't." },
            { t: "Measure. Don't just modify.", b: "Every tuner flips registry keys. Ours ships live DPC sparklines, latency probes, PCIe diagnostics. The 'after' chart isn't taken on faith. Trust, then verify." },
            { t: 'Iterate until you\'d defend it.', b: 'No version one ever ships first try. The fifth attempt is the one that earns the spot. The discount on conviction is what made every other tool in this category mediocre — we don\'t take it.' },
          ].map((p) => (
            <div class="reveal-on-scroll relative pl-6">
              <span class="absolute left-0 top-1.5 bottom-1.5 w-px bg-gradient-to-b from-[var(--color-magenta)] via-[var(--color-cyan)] to-transparent"></span>
              <h3 class="text-[var(--color-text)] text-[19px] font-semibold mb-2 tracking-[-0.01em]">{p.t}</h3>
              <p class="text-[15.5px] leading-[1.7] text-[var(--color-text-mid)]">{p.b}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h2 class="reveal-on-scroll text-[var(--color-text)] text-[28px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] pt-6 leading-tight">
          The suite
        </h2>

        <p class="reveal-on-scroll">
          Optimizationmaxxing tunes your PC.
          Discordmaxxer cuts the comms client down to a chat client.
          Clipmaxxer matches your VOD to today's TikTok.
          Dropmaxxer picks your spots.
          Aimmaxxer drills your gap.
          Viewmaxxing watches your stream's audience graph.
          Editmaxxing handles one mechanic, scoped tight.
        </p>

        <p class="reveal-on-scroll">
          Different surfaces. <span class="serif-accent text-[var(--color-text)]">Same standard.</span>
          Built by a single player for the players who count their frame times.
        </p>

        <p class="reveal-on-scroll pt-6">
          <span class="slogan">made by diggy</span>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="reveal-on-scroll mt-16 pt-10 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
        <a href="/#suite" class="btn-primary">
          Browse the suite
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M5 12h14M13 5l7 7-7 7"/>
          </svg>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
